  <h1>Localized-Orbital Methods</h1>
  <p class="lede">Localized-orbital methods represent electronic states with functions attached to atoms, bonds, fragments, cells, or adaptive spatial regions.</p>
  <p>The category includes empirical tight-binding models, Gaussian or Slater-type basis calculations, numerical atomic orbitals, all-electron atom-centred methods, and mixed formulations. Sharing a localized representation does not make these methods equivalent in Hamiltonian, accuracy, or evidence.</p>

  <h2>Atom-centred functions define a finite subspace</h2>
  <p>A molecular orbital or Kohn–Sham state may be expanded as</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="psi i equals sum mu C mu i chi mu">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo></mo></munderover><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_i(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)=\sum_\mu C_&#123;\mu i&#125;\chi_\mu(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;).</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The functions may differ in angular momentum, radial shape, confinement radius, exponent, centre, and number of radial functions per channel. “Double-zeta polarized” or “tier 2” is meaningful only within a specified basis family and code.</p>
  <p>Locality can reduce matrix sparsity and support chemical interpretation, but it does not guarantee completeness, transferability, or linear scaling for every operation.</p>

  <h2>Nonorthogonal bases produce generalized eigenproblems</h2>
  <p>Atom-centred functions usually overlap. The coefficients therefore satisfy</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="H C equals S C epsilon">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">H</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">C</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">S</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">C</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">ε</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.5em"/><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>⟨</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo>⟩</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;H&#125;\mathbf&#123;C&#125;=\mathbf&#123;S&#125;\mathbf&#123;C&#125;\boldsymbol&#123;\varepsilon&#125;,\quad S_&#123;\mu\nu&#125;=\langle\chi_\mu|\chi_\nu\rangle.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Near-linear dependence makes the overlap matrix ill-conditioned. Adding diffuse or redundant functions can therefore worsen numerical stability even while enlarging the nominal basis.</p>
  <p>Population analyses, projected densities of states, and orbital weights must account for the overlap metric. Coefficient magnitudes alone are not invariant orbital populations.</p>

  <h2>Periodic crystals use Bloch sums of local functions</h2>
  <p>For a basis function <math class="math-inline" aria-label="chi mu"><semantics><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_\mu</annotation></semantics></math> repeated by lattice translations <math class="math-inline" aria-label="R"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;R&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>, a Bloch-adapted basis is</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="chi mu k equals sum R exponential i k dot R chi mu translated">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt></mfrac><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo></mo></munderover><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_&#123;\mu\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)=\frac&#123;1&#125;&#123;\sqrt&#123;N&#125;&#125;\sum_\mathbf&#123;R&#125;e^&#123;i\mathbf&#123;k&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;\chi_\mu(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;-\mathbf&#123;R&#125;).</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The Hamiltonian and overlap matrices become <math class="math-inline" aria-label="k dependent"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mtext>-dependent</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;\text&#123;-dependent&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>. Basis convergence and Brillouin-zone sampling remain separate numerical questions.</p>

  <h2>Empirical tight binding and first-principles local orbitals are distinct</h2>
  <p>In empirical tight binding, selected onsite and hopping parameters define a model Hamiltonian, often fitted to experiment or higher-level calculations. In a first-principles localized-basis calculation, matrix elements are evaluated from an explicit Hamiltonian, density, basis, integration grid, and core treatment.</p>
  <p>Both can use the same algebraic form. Their claims differ: a fitted model may reproduce selected bands without being a transferable total-energy method, while a self-consistent method must still demonstrate basis, grid, sampling, and model convergence.</p>

  <h2>Wannier functions localize a selected Bloch subspace</h2>
  <p>Atom-centred basis functions define the finite representation used to solve a problem. Canonical Kohn–Sham orbitals are eigenstates of the chosen effective Hamiltonian. Wannier functions are a third object: localized transforms of a selected set of Bloch states. A schematic construction is</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Wannier function n R equals one over square root N k sum k exponential minus i k dot R sum m U m n of k psi m k">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mo>|</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⟩</mo><mo>=</mo>
          <mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac>
          <munderover><mo>∑</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo></mo></munderover>
          <msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup>
          <munderover><mo>∑</mo><mi>m</mi><mo></mo></munderover>
          <msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo>
          <mo>|</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⟩</mo><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|w_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;\rangle=\frac&#123;1&#125;&#123;\sqrt&#123;N_k&#125;&#125;\sum_\mathbf&#123;k&#125;e^&#123;-i\mathbf&#123;k&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;\sum_m U_&#123;mn&#125;(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;)|\psi_&#123;m\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;\rangle.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The matrices <math class="math-inline" aria-label="U of k"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">U</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;U&#125;(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;)</annotation></semantics></math> choose a gauge within the selected subspace. For entangled bands, the outer and inner windows, disentanglement, projections, symmetry constraints, and k mesh become part of the construction. A localized Wannier function is therefore not automatically an atomic orbital, a unique chemical bond, or an observable.</p>
  <p>Wannier interpolation must reproduce the target first-principles bands and matrix elements over the stated window before it supports dense-mesh conclusions. Topological bands can obstruct a symmetry-preserving, exponentially localized Wannier representation of the isolated occupied subspace. These gauge and topology questions continue on <a href={href('/theory/berry-phases-and-electronic-topology/')}>Berry Phases and Electronic Topology</a>.</p>

  <h2>Basis families trade systematic simplicity for compactness</h2>
  <dl class="paired-definitions">
    <dt>Gaussian-type orbitals</dt>
    <dd>Enable efficient analytic integral machinery and mature hierarchical basis families, especially in molecular quantum chemistry.</dd>
    <dt>Slater-type orbitals</dt>
    <dd>Have physically natural cusp and asymptotic forms but less convenient multicentre integral structure.</dd>
    <dt>Numerical atomic orbitals</dt>
    <dd>Use tabulated radial functions with flexible confinement and can treat molecules and solids compactly.</dd>
    <dt>Numeric atom-centred all-electron functions</dt>
    <dd>Resolve all electrons with radial functions and numerical integration, avoiding a pseudopotential but requiring near-nucleus accuracy.</dd>
    <dt>Mixed bases</dt>
    <dd>Combine local functions with plane waves, grids, or auxiliary bases to separate difficult spatial or algebraic components.</dd>
  </dl>
  <p>No family is universally best. Efficiency and convergence depend on system type, core treatment, observable, dimensionality, diffuse states, response order, and implementation.</p>

  <h2>Basis convergence is not one scalar setting</h2>
  <p>Relevant choices include cardinality, polarization functions, diffuse functions, angular-momentum cutoff, radial extent, confinement, auxiliary basis, integration grid, and treatment of empty regions. The target observable determines which changes matter.</p>
  <p>Basis-set superposition error occurs when fragments borrow each other’s functions, lowering the combined-system energy more than the separated reference. Counterpoise or larger-basis checks can diagnose this in suitable contexts, but they do not remove every representation or geometry inconsistency.</p>

  <h2>Moving bases create Pulay terms</h2>
  <p>When basis functions depend on nuclear positions or cell parameters, derivatives of the basis contribute to forces and stress. A stationary coefficient vector does not make those basis derivatives vanish in an incomplete representation.</p>
  <p>Force convergence can therefore be slower or different from total-energy convergence. Geometry optimization must test the observable actually used to stop the relaxation.</p>

  <h2>Locality enables sparse and reduced-order methods</h2>
  <p>In insulating systems, density matrices and suitably chosen Wannier representations can decay in real space. This supports truncation, sparse algebra, embedding, fragment methods, and some linear-scaling algorithms.</p>
  <p>Metallicity, small gaps, finite electronic temperature, long-range exchange, diffuse states, response functions, and topological obstructions can weaken or qualify locality. Sparse storage alone does not establish asymptotic linear scaling or controlled error.</p>

  <h2>Study routes</h2>
  <p><a href="https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/electronic-structure/localized-orbitals-tightbinding/A40B1291E9099D574131ACCE2E55816E">Richard M. Martin, <cite>Electronic Structure</cite>, 2nd ed., Chapters 14–15</a> connects localized atomic orbitals, tight-binding models, nonorthogonal matrices, Gaussian and numerical bases, and full self-consistent methods. Full text may require institutional access.</p>
  <p><a href="https://www.materialscloud.org/learn/sections/kpbmzt/wannier90-schools">Materials Cloud Learn / Wannier90 community, <cite>Wannier90 schools</cite></a> is the maintained course-level route missing from the earlier page. The archive groups video recordings and educational material from the 2022 ICTP summer school, the 2022 developers meeting, and the 2020 virtual school, allowing readers to progress from Wannier-function theory and gauge choice to disentanglement, symmetry, topology, automation, and hands-on Wannier90 workflows. Event-specific software releases, Quantum Mobile images, input files, and exercises are version-bound; following them does not validate the chosen band window, subspace, localization, interpolation, or topological compatibility in a new calculation.</p>
  <p><a href="https://docs.siesta-project.org/projects/siesta/en/stable/tutorials/basic/basis-optimization/">SIESTA basis-set optimization tutorial</a> demonstrates numerical atomic-orbital cardinality, confinement, optimization, and practical basis errors. It is implementation documentation, not a universal basis prescription.</p>
  <p><a href="https://fhi-aims.org/uploads/manual/Ch3/S4.html">FHI-aims manual: specifying the basis</a> documents numerical atom-centred all-electron functions and convergence controls. Version-specific manual details must be recorded with production calculations.</p>
  <p><a href="https://molssi-bse.github.io/basis_set_exchange/usage.html">Basis Set Exchange’s basic-usage documentation</a> shows how to retrieve a named Gaussian basis together with version and primary-reference metadata. It is most useful after a reader understands contractions, polarization, and diffuse functions: it supports reproducible identification and format conversion, but does not decide whether a molecular or periodic calculation has reached the basis-set limit.</p>

  <h2>Boundary of this page</h2>
  <p>This page does not equate localized orbitals with empirical tight binding, does not prescribe a universal zeta level or cutoff radius, and does not treat basis labels from different codes as interchangeable. Convergence must be demonstrated for the stated system and observable.</p>
</BaseLayout>
